  - name: Solar quotation checklist
eyebrow: Guide · Quotation
h1: How to build a solar quotation that survives approval.
lead: A quotation that is fast but wrong costs margin, and one that is right but slow loses the customer. The answer is a repeatable process where materials come from rules, prices come from a catalogue and someone approves before it goes out.
answer: Start from the survey, derive the bill of materials from rules, price it from one catalogue, have an approver check margin, and send a proposal that shows savings and payback clearly.
published: 2026-10-11
sections:
  - h: Start from the survey, not from memory
    p:
      - Pull system size, roof facts and electrical details from the survey. When the quotation starts from facts on file, the design does not change halfway through the sale.
  - h: Derive the bill of materials from rules
    p:
      - Most of a solar bill of materials follows from the system size. Panels follow capacity, structure follows panel count, cable follows distance and protection follows string layout.
      - Writing these as rules means two people quoting the same house produce the same list, and a new colleague does not need ten years of experience to quote.
    list:
      - Fixed items that every job needs
      - Items that scale per kilowatt
      - Items that follow panel or string count
      - Items that follow cable length
      - Manual items for the unusual parts of the job
  - h: Price from one catalogue
    p:
      - Keep brands, specifications, units and rates in one catalogue. When prices change, update them once, and every new quotation uses the current rate.
      - Record price history, so you can explain why an older quotation used an older rate.
  - h: Control who sees cost and margin
    p:
      - Sales staff need the selling price. They rarely need the cost price. Limit internal costing to the people who set margin, so negotiations do not erode it by accident.
  - h: Approve before it goes out
    p:
      - Set an approval step for quotations above a size or below a margin you choose. The approver should see the bill of materials, the margin and the survey side by side.
    list:
      - What was surveyed
      - What is being quoted
      - What margin results
      - Who approved and when
  - h: Keep every revision
    p:
      - Customers ask for changes. Keep each revision with its date and reason, so you can show exactly what changed between versions.
  - h: Send a proposal the customer can follow
    p:
      - The proposal is a sales document. Show the system, the materials, the price, the expected savings and the payback in plain language, with your branding.
takeaways:
  - Bill of materials from rules, not from memory.
  - One catalogue, one price history.
  - Approval before sending, and every revision kept.
